        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r249783266-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "A must visit for all Hindus",
        "text": "Normally Hindu temples are chaotic.However Pashupatinathji is different.No wonder it is one part of the group of World Heritage Sites in Kathmandu.If you visit with a vehicle there is a parking lot.You then walk through a road to the temple with shops selling Puja articles.Good thing is that as in most Hindu temples the shopkeepers do not pester you.The temple is spacious and the premises are well maintained mostly.You take off your shoes which are stored for free(look for dirty bags lying around to put your shoes in).You are then expected to wash your hands at rather dirty wash basins before entering the temple.Non Hindus are banned from entry(they are allowed to see the temple across the Baghmati river) .Also mobiles and cameras are not allowed.The temples open quite early and there are two queues to the inner sanctum.Security guards do not allow you more than a few seconds in front of the deity.The architecture of the temple itself is very impressive.Be prepared for a big crowd and people just hanging around.If you are up for it ,after visiting the temple ,walk across to the Baghmati river and you are sure to witness a cremation.The river itself is dirty as the remannts of the dead bodies possibly are thrown in it.There is a move however to clean the river.There are other temples in the sprawling premises where you can go to and exit the temple premises through another get on the other side of the gate by which you entered.Unlike most Hindu temples priests do not pester you in the temple.You feel good visiting the temple.",
